-- **Where.** The schema-driven forms (below), `JsonSchemaForm` (New run and the waiting-for-input form), the node dialog's id and label and its Connect form, the loop panel, Loops create and import, Settings (model form and Enabled switch, the classifier form and its switches, defaults, secrets, API keys), and the API key panel. `JsonSchemaForm` marks the properties in the JSON Schema's `required` list and uses the same controls, with one difference: it starts empty and sends only what it shows, so a boolean is never a switch (always on or off) but **Yes** / **No** segments, after **Not set** unless required (a required one starts with neither chosen). An enum of two to four members is a segmented control (a required one starts with nothing chosen), and a longer one a select ("(choose)" when required, **Not set** otherwise); an enum's empty string reads `""`, apart from **Not set**. A default shows as a placeholder while the field is absent (strings, numbers) or as help ("Default: true"). Its ids are generated per field, so a property may have any name. Each problem shows beside its property under the property's title ("Approval is required", "Reason must NOT have fewer than 3 characters", "Labels: invalid JSON (…)", "Count: enter a number"), located through `validateJson`'s `issues` in `domain` (path segments, a missing property at itself); editing a field clears its problems. It sends what was entered (#235): a property nobody touched is absent; edited text is sent as typed, and cleared text as `""` (its placeholder then reads `""`), for the schema to accept or refuse; an edited number or JSON field must hold a number or JSON, so a blank one, or a number the browser holds as incomplete (`-`, `1e`), is a problem beside it, never left out. An edited optional text, number or JSON field shows **Omit** (named "Omit" and the field's title) at the end of its label's line, before any language tag: a ghost button drawn 20 px high, so it appears without moving the field or